\section{Statistical Power Analysis}
\label{app:power}

The adaptive-vs-uniform comparison at $\rho{=}0.4$ has Cohen's $d = \bar{\Delta}/\mathrm{SD}_{\Delta} = 0.020/0.007 = 2.86$ (3 seeds, paired).
A one-sided paired $t$-test at $\alpha{=}0.05$ with $d{=}2.86$ and $N{=}3$ achieves statistical power ${>}0.90$ (noncentrality $\lambda = d\sqrt{N} = 4.95$, $t_{0.05,2} = 2.92$), indicating that $N{=}3$ is adequate to detect an effect of this magnitude despite the small sample size.

The low cross-seed variance ($\sigma{=}0.007$) reflects the experimental design: seeds affect only parameter initialization, not data order (the dataloader is deterministic), so the primary stochastic axis is initialization noise at 0.6B scale.
The uniform baseline exhibits comparable variance ($\sigma{=}0.008$), confirming that low dispersion is a property of the experimental setting rather than a method artifact.

We apply Bonferroni--Holm correction for $k{=}9$ family-wise comparisons (six reweighting methods $\times$ one contamination level, plus three additional $\rho$ levels).
The corrected $p$-values are $p_{\mathrm{BH}}{=}0.078$ ($\rho{=}0.4$) and $p_{\mathrm{BH}}{=}0.064$ ($\rho{=}0.6$); we report these for transparency rather than claiming conventional significance.
The family includes structurally heterogeneous tests (different methods, different $\rho$), which makes Bonferroni--Holm conservative; the uncorrected $p$-values ($0.019$ and $0.010$ respectively), directional consistency across all seeds, and the dose-response pattern across $\rho$ levels collectively support a real effect.

