\paragraph{The epoch confound in D\&R evaluation.}
The epoch confound identified in \S\ref{sec:main-results} accounts for ${>}$95\% of D\&R's reported advantage; the residual ($\Delta{=}0.017$, 3 seeds) falls within cross-seed variance and is robust to LR schedule choice ($\Delta{=}0.006$ under independent schedules).
A permuted-score control ($+$0.26 vs.\ uniform) confirms that detector signal is load-bearing.

\paragraph{Downstream tasks do not reflect PPL improvements.}
The boundary conditions above frame the practical significance of the observed improvements.
Despite consistent holdout PPL improvements across seeds ($\Delta{=}{-}0.020$ at $\rho{=}0.4$), downstream benchmark accuracy is identical between adaptive and uniform training (Table~\ref{tab:downstream_main}).

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{When uniform beats reweighting.}
Table~\ref{tab:decision} summarizes three conditions, each necessary for effective reweighting: (i)~sufficient detector discrimination \emph{and} calibration (AUC\,$\geq$\,0.9, ECE\,$\leq$\,0.05; \S\ref{sec:robustness}); (ii)~contamination $\rho \geq 0.4$, below which the method degrades ($+$0.046 at $\rho{=}0.2$) with a sharp transition in $[0.3, 0.4]$ (Figure~\ref{fig:delta_vs_rho}, Appendix~\ref{app:rho03}); (iii)~generator--trainee mismatch (at 1.7B the benefit diminishes to $\Delta{=}{-}0.013$ with OOD mixed).
When any condition is violated, uniform is the safer default.

\paragraph{Effect size and downstream impact.}
The in-distribution PPL improvement ($\Delta{=}{-}0.020$ at $\rho{=}0.4$) operates within a narrow residual (${\sim}$0.07-point window between uniform and grid-best), and does not transfer to downstream accuracy: mean accuracy is identical (0.5414 vs.\ 0.5414, 3 seeds, 5 tasks; Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}), consistent with nonlinear PPL-to-accuracy transfer~\citep{tay2023scale,ye2025mixing}.
After accounting for the epoch confound, the paradigm's impact at current scales is modest (Appendix~\ref{app:power}); at $\rho{=}0.6$ the effect roughly doubles ($\Delta{=}{-}0.032$) but this extrapolation is speculative.

